    "q-223794": {
      "dom_id": "step-q223794",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "In **ascending order**, the solutions to $4{g}^{2}=\\frac{2g}{5}$ are $g=${{field-1}} and $g=$\n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and then factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{g}^{2} & =\\frac{2g}{5} \\\\ 4{g}^{2}-\\frac{2g}{5} & =0 \\\\ 2g(2g-\\frac{1}{5}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the zero product rule to get\n\n$$\n2g=0\\,\\text{or}\\,2g-\\frac{1}{5}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\ng=0\\,\\text{or}\\,g=\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $g=0$ and $g=\\frac{1}{10}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
